BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 488.4 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 463.2 Tm (What is the difference between putting a condition in the ON clause versus the WHERE clause) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 449.7 Tm (of a LEFT JOIN?) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 420.2 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 395.0 Tm (For an INNER JOIN the two are equivalent - the optimiser will even rewrite one into the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 381.5 Tm (other. For an outer join they mean completely different things:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 363.0 Tm (- ON is evaluated while matching rows. A non-matching right row simply becomes NULL,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 349.9 Tm (and the left row survives.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 336.4 Tm (- WHERE is evaluated after the join has produced its rows. The NULL-extended row is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 323.3 Tm (then tested, fails the predicate, and the left row disappears.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 304.8 Tm (Net effect: **a filter on the right table's column in WHERE silently turns your LEFT JOIN) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 291.3 Tm (into an INNER JOIN.** This is one of the most common SQL bugs in production reporting.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 272.8 Tm (The exception is WHERE right_col IS NULL, which is the deliberate anti-join idiom: keep) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 259.3 Tm (only left rows that found no match.) Tj ET
