BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 717.0 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 691.8 Tm (What is an index? Explain clustered versus non-clustered indexes and when an index hurts.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 662.3 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 637.1 Tm (An index is an auxiliary data structure - almost always a B+ tree - that maps column values) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 623.6 Tm (to row locations, turning an O\(n\) table scan into an O\(log n\) lookup.) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 600.1 Tm (                     [ 50 | 100 ]                <- root) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 590.1 Tm (                    /      |      \\) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 580.1 Tm (          [10|30]      [60|80]      [120|150]    <- internal) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 570.1 Tm (          /  |  \\       /  |  \\      /   |   \\) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 560.1 Tm (        leaf pages, sorted, doubly linked ----->  <- leaves hold keys + row pointers) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 539.1 Tm (Clustered index - defines the physical order of the table's rows; the leaf level is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 525.6 Tm (the table. One per table. In SQL Server the PRIMARY KEY is clustered by default; in MySQL) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 512.1 Tm (InnoDB it is the primary key, always. Range scans are extremely fast because rows are adjacent) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 498.6 Tm (on disk.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 480.1 Tm (Non-clustered \(secondary\) index - a separate structure holding the key plus a pointer to the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 466.6 Tm (row. Many per table. Requires a second step to fetch the row unless the index covers the query.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 448.1 Tm (PostgreSQL is the important exception: it has no clustered indexes. All indexes are) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 434.6 Tm (secondary, pointing at a heap; the CLUSTER command is a one-time physical reorder that is not) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 421.1 Tm (maintained.) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 397.6 Tm (                 Clustered              Non-clustered) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 387.3 Tm (-----------------------------------------------------) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 377.1 Tm (Count per table  1                      many) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 366.8 Tm (Stores           the rows themselves    key + row pointer) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 356.6 Tm (Extra lookup     none                   yes, unless covering) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 346.3 Tm (Range queries    fastest                slower) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 336.1 Tm (Insert cost      can cause page splits  index maintenance only) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 313.8 Tm (When an index hurts:) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 295.3 Tm (- Every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE must also update every affected index - write) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 282.2 Tm (amplification. A table with 12 indexes writes 13 structures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 268.7 Tm (- Low-cardinality columns \(a boolean, a two-value status\) are usually not worth it; the planner) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 255.6 Tm (will prefer a sequential scan anyway.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 242.1 Tm (- Small tables: a scan of 200 rows beats index overhead.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 229.0 Tm (- Unused indexes still cost writes, storage and backup time.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 215.9 Tm (- Wrapping the column in a function \(WHERE LOWER\(email\) = ...\) makes a plain index unusable -) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 202.9 Tm (you need an expression index.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 173.4 Tm (Example) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 148.2 Tm (orders is queried by customer plus date constantly. A composite index serves both the filter) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 134.7 Tm (and the sort.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 105.2 Tm (Code Example) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 75.0 Tm (-- Composite index: column order matters. Leftmost-prefix rule.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 1 0 0 1 276.9 32 Tm 0.45 g (Page 41 of 160) Tj ET 0 g
