BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 310.4 Tm (On a seven-row table Postgres may still choose a sequential scan - correctly, because it is) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 296.9 Tm (cheaper. Demonstrating awareness of that is a plus, not a failure.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 267.4 Tm (Why Interviewers Ask This) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 242.2 Tm (Indexing is where SQL knowledge meets production reality. Anyone can say "indexes make queries) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 228.7 Tm (faster"; the signal is in the costs, the leftmost-prefix rule, and knowing that Postgres) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 215.2 Tm (has no clustered index.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 185.7 Tm (Common Mistakes) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 160.5 Tm (- "Add an index to every column." This is a write-performance disaster.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 147.4 Tm (- Not knowing composite index column order matters - \(a, b\) serves WHERE a, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 134.3 Tm (WHERE a AND b, but not WHERE b alone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 120.8 Tm (- Claiming Postgres primary keys are clustered.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 107.7 Tm (- Forgetting that a function on the indexed column disables the index.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 94.6 Tm (- Ignoring index bloat and never checking pg_stat_user_indexes.) Tj ET
