BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 386.7 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 361.5 Tm (Explain super key, candidate key, primary key, composite key, unique key and foreign key.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 332.0 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 306.8 Tm (A super key is any set of columns that uniquely identifies a row - including redundant ones.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 293.3 Tm (A candidate key is a minimal super key \(remove any column and uniqueness breaks\). The) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 279.8 Tm (designer picks one candidate key as the primary key; the rest become alternate keys,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 266.3 Tm (usually enforced with UNIQUE. A composite key is any key made of two or more columns. A) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 252.8 Tm (foreign key references a key in another table and enforces referential integrity.) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 229.3 Tm (Key      Unique  Allows NULL                       Count per table) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 219.1 Tm (------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 208.8 Tm (Primary  yes     no                                1) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 198.6 Tm (Unique   yes     yes \(multiple NULLs in PG/MySQL\)  many) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 188.3 Tm (Foreign  no      yes                               many) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 166.1 Tm (In our schema: customers.customer_id is the primary key, customers.email is a candidate key) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 152.6 Tm (enforced as UNIQUE, {customer_id, email} is a super key but not a candidate key, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 139.1 Tm (order_items\(order_id, product_id\) is a composite primary key.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 109.6 Tm (Example) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 84.4 Tm (order_items needs both columns to identify a line: order 1 contains two different products.) Tj ET
