BT /F2 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 349.9 Tm (Question 25) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 317.6 Tm (Difficulty: Hard) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 304.1 Tm (Category: SQL -> Locks, deadlocks) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 274.6 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 249.4 Tm (What is a deadlock? Show how one forms and how you prevent it.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 219.9 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 194.7 Tm (A deadlock is a cycle of lock waits: transaction A holds a lock B needs, and B holds a lock A) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 181.2 Tm (needs. Neither can proceed. The database detects the cycle and kills one transaction \(the) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 167.7 Tm (victim\) so the other completes. Postgres raises SQLSTATE 40P01.) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 144.2 Tm (   T1 holds row 1 ---- waits for ----> row 2 held by T2) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 134.2 Tm (        ^                                    |) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 62 124.2 Tm (        |________ waits for row 1 ___________|) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 103.2 Tm (Lock types worth naming: row-level \(FOR UPDATE exclusive, FOR SHARE shared\) and table-level) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 89.7 Tm (\(ACCESS SHARE for SELECT up to ACCESS EXCLUSIVE for DROP/ALTER\). Deadlocks come mostly) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 76.2 Tm (from inconsistent ordering of row updates across code paths, and from lock escalation caused) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 1 0 0 1 276.9 32 Tm 0.45 g (Page 62 of 160) Tj ET 0 g
