BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 728.0 Tm (1. Find the query. pg_stat_statements ordered by total_exec_time - optimise what actually) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 714.9 Tm (costs the most, not what feels slow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 701.4 Tm (2. Get the real plan. EXPLAIN \(ANALYZE, BUFFERS\). ANALYZE executes it and reports actual) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 688.3 Tm (rows and time; BUFFERS shows cache hits versus disk reads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 674.8 Tm (3. Compare estimated versus actual rows. A large mismatch means bad statistics - the root) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 661.7 Tm (cause of most bad plan choices. Fix with ANALYZE, a higher statistics target, or extended) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 648.2 Tm (statistics for correlated columns.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 634.7 Tm (4. Find the expensive node. Read the plan inside-out; look for the node where actual time) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 621.6 Tm (jumps.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 608.1 Tm (5. Check the access method. Seq Scan on a large table with a selective filter means a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 595.0 Tm (missing or unusable index. On a small table it is correct.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 581.5 Tm (6. Check the join strategy. Nested Loop is great for few rows, catastrophic for many. Hash) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 568.4 Tm (Join suits large unsorted sets; Merge Join suits pre-sorted input.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 554.9 Tm (7. Look for spills. Sort Method: external merge Disk: 240MB means work_mem is too small.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 541.8 Tm (8. Fix and re-measure. Index, rewrite, or configuration - then verify with the same plan.) Tj ET
