BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 337.2 Tm (- Hash - by a hash of a key, for even distribution when there is no natural range.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 319.1 Tm (Benefits: pruning reduces scanned data, maintenance operates per partition, and dropping old) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 305.6 Tm (data becomes DROP TABLE \(instant\) instead of DELETE \(slow, bloating\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 287.1 Tm (Sharding splits data across different database servers. It is the answer to running out of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 273.6 Tm (one machine, and it brings cross-shard joins, distributed transactions, and rebalancing pain.) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 250.1 Tm (                       Partitioning                         Sharding) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 239.8 Tm (--------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 229.6 Tm (Scope                  one server                           many servers) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 219.3 Tm (Solves                 scan volume, maintenance, retention  capacity and throughput ceilings) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 209.1 Tm (Cross-partition query  transparent                          application or proxy must fan out) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 198.8 Tm (Transactions           normal ACID                          distributed, or avoided) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 188.6 Tm (Operational cost       low                                  high) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 166.3 Tm (Rule: partition first, shard only when a single node genuinely cannot cope.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 147.8 Tm (The partition key must appear in the WHERE clause for pruning to happen, and it must be part) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 134.3 Tm (of the primary key.) Tj ET
