BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 717.0 Tm (Why Interviewers Ask This) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 691.8 Tm (Partitioning is where SQL meets system design, and the partition-key discipline \(prune or scan) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 678.3 Tm (everything\) is a concrete, testable insight. It also leads naturally into sharding, which is a) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 664.8 Tm (system design conversation.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 635.3 Tm (Common Mistakes) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 610.1 Tm (- Partitioning without including the partition key in queries, so nothing prunes and you have) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 597.0 Tm (added complexity for nothing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 583.5 Tm (- Omitting the partition column from the primary key - Postgres rejects it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 570.4 Tm (- Creating hundreds of partitions and hitting planning-time overhead.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 557.3 Tm (- Forgetting a DEFAULT partition, so an out-of-range insert fails.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 544.2 Tm (- Confusing partitioning with sharding in an interview - they solve different problems.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 515.1 Tm (Best Practices) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 489.9 Tm (- Choose the partition key from the dominant query filter, usually a date.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 476.8 Tm (- Automate partition creation ahead of time \(pg_partman or a scheduled job\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 463.7 Tm (- Keep partition counts in the tens to low hundreds.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 450.6 Tm (- Use DETACH CONCURRENTLY then DROP for retention, avoiding long locks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 437.5 Tm (- Global uniqueness across partitions is not enforced - design around it.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 408.4 Tm (Follow-up Questions) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 383.2 Tm (1. Why must the partition key be part of the primary key?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 370.2 Tm (2. What is partition-wise join and when does the planner use it?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 357.1 Tm (3. How do you pick a shard key, and what makes a bad one?) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 344.0 Tm (4. Compare range, list and hash partitioning for a multi-tenant SaaS.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.7 Tf 1 0 0 1 66 330.9 Tm (5. How would you migrate a 2 TB live table to a partitioned one with no downtime?) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 301.8 Tm (Real-world Scenario) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 276.6 Tm (An events table reached 1.4 billion rows. The nightly DELETE of 90-day-old data ran for six) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 263.1 Tm (hours, bloated the table, and starved autovacuum. Converting to monthly range partitions turned) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 249.6 Tm (retention into a one-second DROP TABLE, and typical dashboard queries dropped from 22 seconds) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 236.1 Tm (to 400 ms purely from pruning.) Tj ET
