BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 728.0 Tm (Category: Node.js -> Streams) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 698.5 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 673.3 Tm (What are Node streams and why use them instead of reading a whole file?) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 643.8 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 618.6 Tm (A stream processes data in chunks rather than loading everything into memory. Reading a 2 GB) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 605.1 Tm (CSV with readFile allocates 2 GB and risks the heap limit; a stream holds only the current chunk) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 591.6 Tm (plus a bounded buffer.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 573.1 Tm (Four types: Readable \(source\), Writable \(sink\), Duplex \(both, e.g. a socket\), and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 559.6 Tm (Transform \(Duplex that modifies data, e.g. gzip\).) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 541.1 Tm (Backpressure is the key concept: if the consumer is slower than the producer, the buffer grows) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 527.6 Tm (until memory is exhausted. write\(\) returning false signals "stop"; pipe/pipeline handle) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 514.1 Tm (this automatically. Hand-rolling data handlers plus write\(\) without honouring the return value) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 500.6 Tm (is how production memory leaks are created.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 482.1 Tm (Always use stream.pipeline \(or pipeline from node:stream/promises\) rather than .pipe\(\):) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 468.6 Tm (pipe does not forward errors or destroy the remaining streams, so a failure mid-chain leaks) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 455.1 Tm (file descriptors.) Tj ET
