BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 424.3 Tm (Explain Big-O, Big-Theta and Big-Omega. Why do we drop constants?) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 394.8 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 369.6 Tm (They bound how cost grows with input size n. Big-O is an upper bound \(worst case\),) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 356.1 Tm (Big-Omega a lower bound \(best case\), Big-Theta a tight bound \(both\). Interviews say "O") Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 342.6 Tm (but usually mean Theta of the worst case.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 324.1 Tm (Constants and lower-order terms are dropped because they do not affect growth: 3n^2 + 500n + 9) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 310.6 Tm (is O\(n^2\) because for large n the quadratic term dominates. Constants still matter in) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 297.1 Tm (practice - an O\(n\) pass with terrible cache locality can lose to an O\(n log n\) one at) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 283.6 Tm (realistic sizes - and saying so is a strong signal.) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 260.1 Tm (Growth      n = 10  n = 1,000  n = 1,000,000) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 249.9 Tm (--------------------------------------------) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 239.6 Tm (O\(1\)        1       1          1) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 229.4 Tm (O\(log n\)    3       10         20) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 219.1 Tm (O\(n\)        10      1,000      1,000,000) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 208.9 Tm (O\(n log n\)  33      10,000     20,000,000) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 198.6 Tm (O\(n^2\)      100     1,000,000  10^12 \(too slow\)) Tj ET
BT /F3 8.2 Tf 1 0 0 1 60 188.4 Tm (O\(2^n\)      1,024   overflow   overflow) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 166.1 Tm (Rule of thumb for coding rounds: about 10^8 simple operations per second. n <= 20 suggests) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 152.6 Tm (exponential or backtracking is acceptable; n <= 10^3 allows O\(n^2\); n <= 10^6 demands) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 139.1 Tm (O\(n log n\) or better.) Tj ET
