BT /F2 22 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 608.0 Tm (SECTION 5: SYSTEM DESIGN) Tj ET
BT /F2 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 543.3 Tm (SD Q1) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 511.0 Tm (Difficulty: Medium) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 497.5 Tm (Category: System Design -> Scalability fundamentals) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 468.0 Tm (Question) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 442.8 Tm (Explain vertical versus horizontal scaling, and the difference between latency and throughput.) Tj ET
BT /F2 12 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 413.3 Tm (Answer) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 388.1 Tm (Vertical scaling \(scale up\) means a bigger machine. Simple, no code changes, no distributed) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 374.6 Tm (problems - but there is a hardware ceiling, cost rises non-linearly, and it remains a single) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 361.1 Tm (point) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 347.6 Tm (of failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 329.1 Tm (Horizontal scaling \(scale out\) means more machines behind a load balancer. Near-unbounded and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 315.6 Tm (fault tolerant, but it forces you to solve state: sessions, caching, data partitioning,) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 302.1 Tm (consistency.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 283.6 Tm (Latency is time per operation \(measure p50, p95, p99 - never the mean, which hides tail) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 270.1 Tm (behaviour\). Throughput is operations per second. They are related but not the same: batching) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 256.6 Tm (raises throughput while raising latency.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 238.1 Tm (Little's Law ties them together: concurrency = throughput x latency. To serve 5,000 requests per) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 1 0 0 1 54 224.6 Tm (second at 200 ms each you need 1,000 concurrent slots.) Tj ET
